CAT = "trcnProtect"; SEG = "PRT"
# Safeguarding: Foundational Principles and Policies; Recognising Signs of Abuse and Reporting.
Q = [
("What is the difference between safeguarding and child protection?",
 "Safeguarding is broader", ["They mean exactly the same", "Child protection is broader", "Safeguarding covers adults only"],
 "Safeguarding covers everything done to keep all children safe and prevent harm. Child protection is the part that responds to a child who is being harmed or is at risk."),
("Under Nigeria's Child Rights Act of 2003, a child is a person under the age of:",
 "18", ["16", "21", "14"],
 "The Child Rights Act 2003 defines a child as a person under 18 years of age. States domesticate the Act through their own laws."),
("A pupil tells her teacher that an adult at home is hurting her. What should the teacher do first?",
 "Listen calmly and reassure her", ["Ask her for every detail", "Promise to keep it a secret forever", "Call the adult to ask"],
 "Listen without pressing for details, reassure the child she was right to tell, and then follow the reporting procedure. Questioning or confronting the adult can harm the child and the case."),
("Why should a teacher never promise to keep a child's disclosure of abuse secret?",
 "It may have to be reported", ["Children often lie about it", "Secrets waste class time", "Parents must know first"],
 "Concerns about abuse must be passed to the designated safeguarding lead or authorities, so a promise of secrecy would be broken and damage trust."),
("A pupil often has bruises on the upper arms, flinches when an adult moves quickly, and gives odd explanations. These may be signs of:",
 "Physical abuse", ["Neglect", "Online abuse", "Exam stress"],
 "Unexplained or repeated injuries in protected areas, flinching and changing stories are possible indicators of physical abuse and should be reported."),
("A pupil regularly arrives hungry, in dirty clothes, and misses school with untreated illnesses. These are possible signs of:",
 "Neglect", ["Physical abuse", "Bullying", "Truancy only"],
 "Neglect is the persistent failure to meet a child's basic needs: food, clothing, hygiene, health care and supervision."),
("Constant name-calling, threats and humiliation of a child by a carer are signs of:",
 "Emotional abuse", ["Neglect", "Physical abuse", "Poor discipline only"],
 "Emotional or psychological abuse is persistent ill-treatment that harms a child's sense of worth, such as humiliation, threats and rejection."),
("After a child discloses abuse, what should the teacher write down?",
 "The child's own words", ["Her opinion of the family", "What she thinks happened", "Only her own feelings"],
 "Record the date, time and the child's words as exactly as possible, plus what was seen, kept factual and confidential."),
("Who should a teacher normally report a safeguarding concern to first?",
 "The safeguarding lead", ["The child's classmates", "A local newspaper", "Other parents"],
 "Concerns go to the school's designated safeguarding lead or head, who follows the referral pathway to social welfare, police or health services as needed."),
("The principle of \"do no harm\" in safeguarding means the teacher's actions must:",
 "Not put the child at more risk", ["Avoid all paperwork", "Protect the school's good image", "Keep the case quiet"],
 "Do no harm means every response, including how information is shared, should avoid exposing the child to further danger or distress."),
("Safeguarding information about a pupil should be shared:",
 "Only with those who need it", ["With the whole staff room", "With the class to warn them", "On the school notice board"],
 "Confidentiality means sharing information on a need-to-know basis with people who can protect the child, never as gossip."),
("A pupil is groomed by an adult who sends gifts and asks for photos through a chat app. This is a form of:",
 "Online abuse", ["Digital literacy", "Normal friendship", "Cyber research"],
 "Online grooming, building trust in order to exploit a child, is a form of online abuse and must be reported."),
("A teacher notices a pupil suddenly withdrawn, avoiding a particular adult and showing knowledge of sex beyond her age. She should:",
 "Report her concern promptly", ["Wait for proof first", "Question the adult herself", "Tell the pupil's friends"],
 "Teachers do not need proof to report a concern. Changes in behaviour and age-inappropriate sexual knowledge are possible signs of sexual abuse."),
("Before photographing pupils for a school brochure, the school should obtain:",
 "Informed consent from parents", ["Approval from the pupils' friends", "Permission from a printer", "Nothing, if it is in school"],
 "Informed consent means parents or guardians understand how images will be used and agree, protecting the child's privacy."),
("Which statement about who can harm children is correct?",
 "Anyone, even trusted adults", ["Only strangers harm children", "Only other children do", "Only people in cities do"],
 "Most abuse is by someone the child knows, such as a relative, carer or trusted adult, so concerns must never be dismissed because of who is involved."),
("Which is a risk factor that makes a child more vulnerable to harm?",
 "Living without parental care", ["Doing well in class", "Playing a sport", "Having many friends"],
 "Children without parental care, with disabilities, or affected by conflict, displacement or poverty face higher risks of abuse and exploitation."),
("A safeguarding record about a pupil should be kept:",
 "Locked away from class files", ["In the class register", "On the staff notice board", "In the pupil's exercise book"],
 "Safeguarding records are confidential: stored securely, separately from general files, with access limited to those who need it."),
("A child who has been through a frightening event such as a flood may need psychosocial support. This means:",
 "Help for emotional well-being", ["Extra homework to keep her busy", "A strict new timetable", "Moving to another school"],
 "Psychosocial support addresses emotional and social well-being, for example through safe routines, listening, play and referral where needed."),
("Pupils in a school near a busy road keep crossing between parked lorries. Identifying this danger and acting on it is part of:",
 "Risk assessment", ["Lesson planning", "Continuous assessment", "Curriculum review"],
 "Risk assessment means identifying hazards, judging who could be harmed and how, and putting controls in place."),
("Forcing a 12-year-old girl to hawk goods on the street during school hours is a form of:",
 "Child labour", ["Vocational training", "Holiday work", "Apprenticeship"],
 "Work that keeps children from school or harms their health or development is child labour, which the Child Rights Act restricts."),
]
